.TH VT 2
.SH NAME
vtnew, vtdumb, vtfree, vtresize, vtreset, vtwrite, vtframe, vtkey, vtpaste, vtmouse, vtfocus, vtwidth \- a terminal's screen, with nothing drawn
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ta \w'void* 'u
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <keyboard.h>
.br
.B #include <vt.h>
.PP
.B
Vt*	vtnew(int cols, int rows)
.PP
.B
Vt*	vtdumb(void (*put)(Vt *vt, Rune *r, int n))
.PP
.B
void	vtfree(Vt *vt)
.PP
.B
void	vtresize(Vt *vt, int cols, int rows)
.PP
.B
void	vtreset(Vt *vt)
.PP
.B
void	vtwrite(Vt *vt, uchar *buf, long n)
.PP
.B
void	vtframe(Vt *vt, Vtframe *f)
.PP
.B
int	vtkey(Vt *vt, int key, char *buf, int n)
.PP
.B
long	vtpaste(Vt *vt, char *s, long len, char *buf, long n)
.PP
.B
int	vtmouse(Vt *vt, int but, int x, int y, int press, char *buf, int n)
.PP
.B
int	vtfocus(Vt *vt, int in, char *buf, int n)
.PP
.B
int	vtwidth(Rune r)
.PP
.B
void*	emalloc(ulong n)
.PP
.B
void*	erealloc(void *p, ulong n)
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions keep the screen a Unix program draws on a terminal by xterm's escape
sequences, so that a program such as
.IR vt (1)
can show it.
They draw nothing: a screen is cells, and what changed is told in frames.
.PP
.I Vtnew
makes a screen of
.I cols
columns and
.I rows
rows;
.I vtfree
frees it.
.I Vtwrite
gives it
.I n
bytes of the program's output, in UTF-8.
A sequence or a rune may be split between writes.
.I Vtresize
changes its size: what fits stays where it was, and if the cursor's row is cut off,
the rows above it leave the top as a line feed would push them.
.I Vtreset
is RIS, a terminal as
.I vtnew
made it.
.PP
.B Vt.line
is the screen shown,
.B Vt.rows
lines of
.B Vt.cols
cells.
A cell holds a rune, its colors and its attributes,
.BR Vbold ,
.BR Vfaint ,
.BR Vitalic ,
.BR Vunder ,
.BR Vblink ,
.BR Vreverse ,
.BR Vinvisible
and
.BR Vstrike .
A rune two columns wide fills two cells: the first has
.BR Vwide ,
the second
.B Vcont
and the rune 0.
A rune of no width, a combining mark, has no cell.
A color is
.BR Vdefault ,
.B Vindex
or'd with one of the 256 colors, or
.B Vrgb
or'd with 0xRRGGBB.
A cell's
.B link
is the index in
.B Vt.links
of the URI an OSC 8 sequence gave it, or 0.
A line's
.B wrapped
says the line runs on into the next, by autowrap.
.PP
.B Vt.x
and
.B Vt.y
are the cursor, shown if
.B Vt.mode
has
.BR Mcursor .
The other modes are
.B Mappcursor
(DECCKM),
.B Mappkeypad
(DECKPAM),
.B Mwrap
(DECAWM),
.B Morigin
(DECOM),
.B Minsert
(IRM),
.B Mnewline
(LNM),
.B Malt
(the alternate screen, by 47, 1047 or 1049),
.B Mbracket
(2004),
.B Mfocus
(1004),
.BR Mmouse ,
.B Mmousedrag
and
.B Mmouseall
(1000, 1002 and 1003), and
.B Msgr
(1006).
.B Vt.title
is the last title OSC 0 or 2 gave.
.PP
.I Vtframe
fills
.I f
with what changed since the last frame, in the order to apply it.
.B all
says every row changed: the screen was switched, resized or reset.
.B ngone
lines left the top of the main screen, by a line feed or SU with the scroll region at
the top; they are
.BR Vt.gone ,
oldest first, at most
.BR Vgone ,
and
.B lost
counts those left out before them.
Then the
.B nscroll
scrolls
.BR scroll :
rows
.B top
to
.B bot\-1
moved up
.B n
rows, or down if
.B n
is negative, the rows uncovered blank.
Then the rows whose byte in
.B dirty
is set changed, numbered as they are after the scrolls.
.B Cursor
says the cursor moved or was shown or hidden,
.B modes
has the modes that changed,
.B title
says the title changed, and
.B bell
counts the BELs.
.BR Scroll ,
.B dirty
and
.B Vt.gone
are the library's, good until the next call that changes the screen.
.PP
.B Vt.reply
is called with the answers to the program's questions (DA, DSR's cursor position),
for the program; if it is nil they are dropped.
.B Vt.aux
is the caller's.
.PP
.IR Vtkey ,
.IR vtpaste ,
.I vtmouse
and
.I vtfocus
put in
.I buf
the bytes to send the program for a key, a paste, the mouse, or the window's focus, as
the modes ask, and return how many, 0 if there are none to send, or \-1 if
.I n
is too few, with the error string set.
A
.I key
is a rune or one of
.IR keyboard.h 's,
.BR Kup ,
.BR Kdown ,
.BR Kleft ,
.BR Kright ,
.BR Khome ,
.BR Kend ,
.BR Kpgup ,
.BR Kpgdown ,
.BR Kins ,
or
.B KF|1
to
.BR KF|12 ,
with
.BR Vctl ,
.B Valt
and
.B Vshift
or'd in;
.B Kbs
sends DEL, and
.B Kdel
the delete key's ESC [3~.
A paste's newlines are sent as returns; with
.B Mbracket
it is bracketed, and its escapes dropped, so it cannot end the bracket.
.IR Vtmouse 's
.I but
is 1, 2 or 3, a button, 4 or 5, the wheel, or 0, none, with the modifiers or'd in;
.I press
is 1 for a press, 0 for a release and \-1 for a move;
.I x
and
.I y
are a cell's.
.PP
.I Vtwidth
gives a rune's columns: 2 for Unicode 15.1's East Asian Wide and Fullwidth, 0 for its
nonspacing and enclosing marks and format characters but the soft hyphen, and for the
controls, and 1 for the rest.
.PP
.I Vtdumb
makes a terminal that keeps no screen, for a program, such as
.IR win ,
that shows text only.
.I Vtwrite
parses as it does for any other, but calls
.I put
with the
.I n
runes of text in
.IR r ,
with the backspaces, tabs, returns and newlines among them, and a vertical tab or form feed
as a newline; a sequence, another control and a C1 rune are dropped.
DEC's line drawing is given as Unicode's, and REP repeats the last rune.
For it,
.IR vtframe 's
frame is empty,
.I vtresize
does nothing, and
.B Vt.reply
is never called.
.SS Memory
The library expects the program to provide two allocation routines,
.I emalloc
and
.IR erealloc .
They are
.IR malloc (2)'s
and
.IR realloc ,
except that they do not return if memory runs out, and
.I emalloc
sets the memory it returns to zero.
The library gets all its memory from them, and gives it back with
.IR free .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libvt
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vt (1)
.SH BUGS
A combining mark is dropped.
Double-width and double-height lines, VT52 and the sixel graphics DCS brings are not
kept.
